<Docs
  title="Use a component — PhoenixSEO Page Builder docs"
  description="How to place a component on a page in the block editor, fill its fields, duplicate an instance, and duplicate a component type into an editable copy."
  path="/docs/use/"
  doc="use"
  lede="Chapter 03"
>
  <h1>Use a component</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Placing a component is one click. Filling it in happens in the sidebar, not in a modal, and the canvas
    shows the real output while you type.
  </p>

  <h2>Place it</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Open the page</strong> in the block editor and press the <code>+</code> button — top left,
        or the one that appears when you hover between two blocks.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Find the category.</strong> <em>PhoenixSEO Components</em> is the first category in the
        inserter, above WordPress's own. It holds one block per component type.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Pick one.</strong> The block appears at the cursor, already rendering — the editor runs the
        same <code>render.php</code> the frontend runs, so what you see is the component, not a preview
        image of it.
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="shot">
    <img
      src="/docs/use-inserter.webp"
      width="450"
      height="890"
      alt="The block inserter with the category PhoenixSEO Components, listing FAQ, Hero, Feature grid, Pricing, Key figures, Record, Movie and Series."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The inserter. A component that ships with the plugin is listed here as soon as the plugin is active;
      one you add yourself appears after a reload.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Fill it in</h2>

  <p>
    Select the block. The right sidebar switches to the <em>Block</em> tab and lists the component's
    fields, in the order the definition gives them. A field marked with an asterisk is required — the
    definition says so, and the plugin does not invent a second rule on top.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <img
      src="/docs/use-fields.webp"
      width="1090"
      height="930"
      alt="A Hero component selected on the canvas, with its fields Heading, Subline, Link text and Target shown in the Block tab of the sidebar."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The fields of <code>hero</code>: heading, subline, and a call to action. Nothing else is exposed,
      because nothing else is in the definition.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Long fields — a repeater's rows, for instance — scroll inside the sidebar rather than growing the page.
    Text is saved with the page, so <em>Publish</em> or <em>Update</em> is the last step, as usual.
  </p>

  <h2>Several instances of one component</h2>

  <p>
    A page can hold any number of instances of the same type; each carries its own values. Two ways to get
    a second one:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Add it again</strong> through the inserter. It starts with the component's defaults.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Duplicate the instance</strong>: the block toolbar's <em>Options</em> menu (⋮) →
      <em>Duplicate</em>, or <code>Ctrl</code>+<code>Shift</code>+<code>D</code>. The copy keeps every value
      of the original, which is what you want when the second one differs in two words.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Instances do not collide: each one lives in the page document under its own id, and the markup is
    rendered per instance. Reordering happens by dragging, or in the list view.
  </p>

  <h2>Duplicate the component, not the instance</h2>

  <p>
    An instance copy changes one page. A type copy changes what every page can place — use it when you want
    a variant that behaves differently, and want to keep the shipped original intact.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Open <em>Page Builder</em></strong> and find the component's row.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Press <em>Duplicate</em></strong> under the row's actions. A field appears for the new type —
        lowercase, digits, hyphens. It has to differ from the original, and it becomes the folder name.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Press <em>Create.</em></strong> The copy is written to
        <code>wp-content/sdc-components/&lt;new-type&gt;/</code> and is editable there. The original stays
        where it was.
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="shot">
    <img
      src="/docs/use-duplicate.webp"
      width="1239"
      height="266"
      alt="The FAQ row in the component catalog with the Duplicate panel open, a New Type field filled with faq-compact and a Create button."
    />
    <figcaption>
      Duplicating <code>faq</code> — a plugin component, therefore read-only — into a new type you own.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>Why a new type and not just an edit</strong>
    A page stores the type it placed. Renaming the type would orphan every instance of it, so duplication
    asks for a name and leaves the original alone. Existing pages keep using the original until you swap the
    block.
  </p>

  <h2>Remove</h2>

  <p>
    Deleting a block from a page removes it there and nowhere else. Deleting a <em>component</em> — in
    <em>Page Builder</em>, only for site components — removes the type for the whole site. The plugin counts
    the instances first and refuses unless you tick <em>Delete even if in use</em>; pages that still
    reference it then render nothing in its place, and <code>wp sdc-pb repair</code> will report them.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>

  <p>
    Ten components may be enough. When they are not, <a href="/docs/build/">chapter 04</a> writes one.
  </p>
</Docs>
